  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates lotteries based on 'probable outcomes,' defined as outcomes with a probability greater than 1 divided by the number of outcomes in the lottery. For Lottery A, there are 3 outcomes, so the threshold for a probable outcome is 1/3 ≈ 33.3%. Thus, outcomes with probability > 33.3% are considered probable. In Lottery A, 2500 Euros (33%) is not greater than 33.3%, so it does not qualify as probable. 2400 Euros (66%) is greater than 33.3%, so it is probable. The 0 Euros outcome (1%) is not probable. Therefore, only 2400 Euros is a probable outcome in Lottery A. In Lottery B, there is one outcome (2400 Euros) with 100% probability. Since there is only one outcome, the threshold is 1/1 = 100%, so only outcomes with probability > 100% would be probable—but no outcome exceeds 100%. Thus, no outcome in Lottery B qualifies as probable under this rule. However, the rule specifies 'the lottery with a higher average of probable outcomes.' Since Lottery A has one probable outcome (2400 Euros), its average of probable outcomes is 2400. Lottery B has zero probable outcomes, so its average is undefined or 0. Therefore, the reason would prefer Lottery A based on the higher average of probable outcomes (2400 > 0). However, this leads to a counterintuitive result, as the rule as defined may not be applicable in cases where no outcome exceeds the threshold. Nonetheless, the reason is formally applicable: it can be evaluated based on the number of probable outcomes and their average. The key point is that the reason is defined and can be applied to the decision problem.",
